{"author":{"id":"85a3c31a64931f83958f26b24045fef8415ea6ff04d942d15661891c77fc0695"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1794217691,"nonce":"gkL+BqOUgQ7CogKV6sPlzitdT84=","message":"Extension trait landed in the working tree (uncommitted). russl_compiler::Extension with four hooks: prelude (a library module every module of the program sees as a glob import; a module's own items shadow it), extern_fn (a shader declares `extern { fn f(..) -\u003e T; }`, or `extern \"russl\"`, and the extension supplies a russl fn with the same signature, parameter names aside; a missing or mismatched one is an error at the declaration), builtins (name, parameter and result types as russl spells them, and a closure that lowers the call to naga expressions through Emit::append), and check (rules over a ProgramView: emitted bindings with group, binding, kind, type and span; entry points; the root's constants). compile_program_ext takes one; the other entry points use NoExtension. Program::compile_ext and russl_build::compile_ext for files.\n\nThe macro's logic moved to a new library crate, russl_expand (Expander { continuation, extension } with expand and resume), so a framework's shader macro is two proc-macro functions around it; russl_macros is now that wrapper with NoExtension. Source macros already call back whatever continuation they are given, so modules written with russl! and with a framework macro import each other; a program compiles with its root's extension.\n\nTests: 6 compiler-level (prelude, shadowing, externs present, missing and mismatched, a builtin, a binding rule, extern without an extension), and a fixture pair through rustc: fixtures/toy_macro (a framework macro with an extension) and fixtures/toy_app (its shader reads the prelude, links the extern, and imports a plain russl! module).\n\nWith generic functions this covers the issue's scope. Generic structs were not part of it; filed separately.","files":null}]}